<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kartlis Tskhovreba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th–14th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as The Georgian Chronicles, Life of Georgia, The Life of Georgia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kartlis Tskhovreba, meaning the Life of Kartli or Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, is a collection of medieval Georgian chronicles that forms the main narrative source for the history of Georgia. Written by different authors between the ninth and fourteenth centuries, it runs from legendary origins through the histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a> to the Mongol period. It was revised and continued in the eighteenth century under King Vakhtang VI.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kartlis-tskhovreba.jpg" alt="Kartlis Tskhovreba" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Buba Kudava · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E1%83%9B%E1%83%90%E1%83%A0%E1%83%98%E1%83%90%E1%83%9B%E1%83%98%E1%83%A1%E1%83%94%E1%83%A3%E1%83%9A%E1%83%98_%E1%83%A5%E1%83%90%E1%83%A0%E1%83%97%E1%83%9A%E1%83%98%E1%83%A1_%E1%83%AA%E1%83%AE%E1%83%9D%E1%83%95%E1%83%A0%E1%83%94%E1%83%91%E1%83%90.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Georgian Chronicles, known in Georgian as Kartlis Tskhovreba, &quot;The Life of Kartli&quot;, are the principal compendium of medieval Georgian historical texts. Kartli was a core region of ancient and medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, known to Classical and Byzantine authors as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. The collection is also called the Georgian Royal Annals, because it was essentially the official corpus of history of the Kingdom of Georgia.</p><p>It consists of a series of distinct texts dating from the 9th to the 14th century. The dating of these works and the identification of their authors, such as Leonti Mroveli and Juansheriani, have long been debated by scholars. The latest texts were added in the 14th century, and a canonical version was edited early in the 18th century by a special commission appointed and chaired by King Vakhtang VI.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first three works, the &quot;History of the Kings and Patriarchs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>&quot;, the &quot;History of King Vakhtang Gorgasali&quot; and the &quot;Martyrdom of Saint Archil&quot;, already formed a corpus by the 11th century. It covered Georgian history from the earliest times through the reign of Vakhtang I Gorgasali down to the death of his descendant Archil in 786.</p><p>In the middle of the 12th century two further texts were added. The &quot;Chronicle of Kartli&quot; records Georgian history from the late 8th century through the reign of Bagrat III, the first all-Georgian king, to the early regnal years of George II. The &quot;History of the King of Kings David&quot; continues the story and focuses on the reign of David IV. The texts preserve evidence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> as well as for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in general, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and the Turks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The surviving Georgian manuscripts are relatively late. The earliest is the Anaseuli or &quot;Queen Anna&quot; codex, dated to 1479-1495, and another major variant, the Mariamiseuli or &quot;Queen Mariam&quot; codex, was copied in 1633-1645/1646. An abridged Armenian adaptation, &quot;The History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>&quot;, most probably made in the 12th century, is older than any of them, though its earliest extant manuscript was copied in 1279-1311. Translations exist in French, Russian, English and German, each covering only some of the texts.</p><p>Some modern scholars, such as Ivane Javakhishvili, have questioned the authenticity of the early components and urged extreme caution, since strict historical facts are frequently intermingled with mythical ones. Critical analyses against other sources, including Classical authors, and recent archaeological studies have nevertheless confirmed the trustworthiness of many of the accounts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Georgian Chronicles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Historians compiled the chronicles later gathered as <b>Kartlis Tskhovreba</b>, the Life of Georgia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Medieval Georgian tradition, preserved in the chronicle collection <b>Kartlis Tskhovreba</b>, credits Pharnavaz I with uniting the land around 300 BCE, after the fall of the Achaemenid Empire and the campaigns of Alexander the Great, and with dividing it into provinces under governors called eristavis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
